# App-facing P2P library

Use `@unstoppable-stack/p2p` for new app networking, RPC and shared data.
Inspect the target's installed exports/types and compatibility profile first.
The `/service`, `/rpc`, `/data` and `/db` APIs require alpha.2 or later. Preserve
older generated apps and migrate explicitly; plugin updates do not migrate apps.
All P2P APIs remain experimental. Personal Node is optional and deferred, not a
prerequisite for apps communicating directly.

| App need | API |
| --- | --- |
| Single-writer shared JSON | `/data`: `openCollection` |
| Collaborative JSON with approved writers | `/data`: `openCollaborativeDatabase` |
| One remote service | `/rpc`: `defineCapability`, `procedure`; `/service`: `serve`, `connect` |
| Several capabilities or bidirectional calls | Root: `createNode`, pairing, grants, `peer.client` / `call` / `stream` |
| Host existing cores, Hyperbees or drives | `/sync`: `syncPack` and resource manifests |
| Custom deterministic collaborative reducer/view | `/db`, with explicit Autobase opt-in |

Read `managed-data.mdc` for data semantics. New service/data apps do not need
manual DHT bootstraps, Hyperswarm, bare-rpc wrappers, SecretStream or Protomux
setup. The library owns transport, identity, permissions and cleanup. Do not
add a second swarm/store around a managed helper. Root imports avoid loading
replication; storage dependencies are still installed with the package.

Apps own UI, domain handlers, validation, schemas and private state. Keep native
sessions in Node or the desktop Bare worker, behind the existing IPC bridge.
Shared RPC contracts are lightweight but contain functions: do not serialize
them through JSON IPC. Expo needs its own native acceptance; do not assume these
helpers work in a browser or mobile worklet.

Permissions differ: scoped RPC invites grant methods, dataset keys grant reads,
and collaborative writer keys require explicit founder admission. Discovering a
peer or binding a typed contract grants no access. Revoking RPC grants does not
revoke data already shared. Keep private stores outside replication.

Use one owner per identity directory and close sessions/nodes/datasets on app
shutdown. A disconnected service session must be closed and explicitly connected
again; calls and mutations are not automatically retried. Honor handler signals,
use bounded waits and app-owned idempotency for mutations.

Direct Holepunch imports remain appropriate for existing storage formats,
app-owned Hyperdrive operations, custom protocols and isolated transport tests.
They are implementation/advanced references, not the default app recipe. Reuse
the existing owner; never silently replace an edited store or its merge policy.
Use installed P2P docs/types before upstream primitive documentation.
